{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological evolution lacks a conscious agent making a 'choice', whereas scientists actively select theories.","In tournaments, the winner is declared best by definition, whereas in science, the survivor is only 'not yet falsified'.","Investment survivorship often ignores the graveyard of failed funds, while Popper explicitly focuses on the falsified ones to learn."],"overturn_conditions":["If the 'harsh conditions' of the past are structurally different from future conditions, the analogy breaks and the choice becomes arbitrary.","If the goal of science is defined as 'finding true theories' rather than 'eliminating false ones', the non-inductive preference fails to meet the goal."],"shared_structure":["Selection based on past survival against harsh conditions","No guarantee of future survival despite past success","Preference for the 'fittest' survivor as the only rational betting strategy available"],"source_memory_refs":["evolutionary_survival_of_species","tournament_elimination_logic","survivorship_bias_investment"],"transfer_claims":["Just as we bet on the species that survived an ice age without claiming it is immune to the next one, we adopt the corroborated theory without claiming it is probable.","The rationality of the choice lies in the quality of the elimination process, not the ontological status of the survivor."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive methodological rule for selecting the most 'testable' (and thus most informative) surviving candidate, but this defense costs him the ability to claim that such theories are more likely to be true or reliable in future applications, reducing scientific rationality to a game of eliminating error rather than approaching truth.","counterconditions":["If surviving severe tests implies any degree of future reliability, the view collapses into induction.","If the preference cannot guide action in novel situations where no past test data exists, the view is practically useless.","If 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) is invoked to justify the preference, it must be shown not to rely on inductive assumptions about the distribution of truth."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument proceeds by distinguishing between 'probability' (inductive support) and 'corroboration' (a report of past test severity). It posits that preferring the better-corroborated theory is not an prediction of future success (which would be induction) but a strategic choice to bet on the theory that has survived the hardest attempts at falsification, thereby maximizing the potential for future learning should it fail. This treats the preference as a 'convention of action' similar to choosing the sharpest knife not because it will definitely cut better tomorrow, but because it has proven most responsive to sharpening and use today.","optional_refs":["7d75144d16851f921bb0094b3d450c55ebdcc1d54132beb008fb275247806956","f78787bd7e7d6bceee833a27e7daa6b1d048b5a86d7fd479d7658a8eda84ee22"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["7d75144d16851f921bb0094b3d450c55ebdcc1d54132beb008fb275247806956","f78787bd7e7d6bceee833a27e7daa6b1d048b5a86d7fd479d7658a8eda84ee22"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}